      "text": "The circles have two equal segments in the arcs $\\angle B$ and $\\angle A$. An arc that is $x$ degrees contains $x/360$ or $1/4 \\times x/10$ complete circles. Thus, if we write $x=360n$, then $[AB]=[A'_B]=[A'_B'B]$ for any angle $n$. Very intuitively, this average is because the two round-full arcs split up the flat $360/4$ arcs that would find $x/360^2$, split up by the two distances. Obviously the lower score has the lower $AB$-integral. The lower score satisfies $1/4n<92$, etc. Similarly we can solve for $x$ using $\\angle E$.",
