      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "First, notice that we can rewrite $113^\\circ$ and $111^\\circ$ as $113^\\circ = 60^\\circ + 53^\\circ$ and $111^\\circ = 57^\\circ + 54^\\circ$.\n\nNow, we can see that the sum of all angles around point $A$ is $360^\\circ$. Let's call each other angle formed as $x$, $y$, $z$, and $w$. Also, we can find the measure of angles $P$, $Q$, and $R$ by subtracting the sum of our new angles ($60^\\circ + 53^\\circ$ and $57^\\circ + 54^\\circ$) from $360^\\circ$. Hence, $x + y + z + w = 360^\\circ - (60^\\circ + 53^\\circ + 57^\\circ + 54^\\circ)$.\n\nRemaining angles, $Q$ and $R$, sum up to $360^\\circ - (60^\\circ + 53^\\circ) - (57^\\circ + 54^\\circ) = 150^\\circ$. Now, $P = Q + R = 150^\\circ - (y + z)$. So, $x + 2y + 2z$ equals $360^\\circ - 150^\\circ = 210^\\circ$.\n\nFinally, $x + 2y + 2z - (P + y + z + 3 \\cdot 92^\\circ)$ gives us $\\boxed{44^\\circ}$.",
